        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "2c3652dd-ad51-5d02-8988-4f645d6f01f0",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Heckscher State Parkway, Great River, Islip Terrace, Long Island, New York, Heckscher State Park, Town of Islip, Suffolk, New York City, Great South Bay, Atlantic Ocean, Fire Island\n- Concepts: parkways, hamlets, census-designated places\n\nFacts:\n- The Heckscher State Parkway, formerly known as the Heckscher Spur, is an 8.24-mile parkway on Long Island in New York, United States.\n- At Islip Terrace, the Heckscher State Parkway turns south, crosses NY 27, and ends at the toll barrier for Heckscher State Park in Great River.\n- Great River is a suburban hamlet and census-designated place in the Town of Islip in Suffolk, New York.\n- Great River is about 50 miles east of New York City, or about 55 miles by road, on the South Shore of Long Island.\n- Great River adjoins the Great South Bay and is protected from the Atlantic Ocean by Fire Island.",
